\section{Resolve an Identity Before Assigning an Address}

Levine's model has two levels of symbol table: each object has its own indexed entries, and the linker builds a global table that relates definitions and references in the participating objects. His diagrams use a historical GNU \texttt{a.out} implementation, but the reason for the indirection still applies to ELF. A relocation naming symbol index 7 in one object need not refer to anything named by index 7 in another; the index is relative to the symbol table associated with that relocation. When emitting a new symbol table, the linker must remap outgoing references to its new indices \cite[chapter~5]{levine2000linkers}. Keep a record of which input supplied each candidate, even after choosing a winner: without that information, a diagnostic can name the conflicting symbol but not the conflicting files.

In ELF an ordinary section-defined symbol in a relocatable object has a section identity and a section-relative value. Binding tells the linker whether that definition participates in cross-file name resolution: \verb|STB_LOCAL| is private to the input object, while \verb|STB_GLOBAL| and \verb|STB_WEAK| participate. A section-relative value of zero is perfectly normal for a definition at the start of a section. Test the section index, not the value, to distinguish \verb|SHN_UNDEF| from a definition. \verb|SHN_ABS| needs no section base, and \verb|SHN_COMMON| describes a storage request rather than an address. See the \href{https://gabi.xinuos.com/elf/05-symtab.html}{ELF generic ABI symbol table rules}.

\section{Precedence, Ties, and Visibility}

For ordinary symbols in explicitly included relocatable inputs, a regular global definition takes precedence over a common request, which in turn takes precedence over a weak definition. GNU ELF links combine common requests by taking the largest size and strictest alignment, independently. For example, \verb|.comm scratch,24,8| and \verb|.comm scratch,16,32| request 24 bytes at 32-byte alignment, not 24 bytes at eight-byte alignment. In ELF, size is in \verb|st_size| and the common symbol's \verb|st_value| encodes alignment. Do not use common coalescing to excuse two ordinary global definitions; GNU \verb|ld.bfd| diagnoses that conflict by default. Also do not infer common from a source declaration alone: modern GCC defaults to \verb|-fno-common| for C.

Two weak definitions do not establish a portable preference. In a simple GNU \verb|ld.bfd| link of ordinary objects, the first wins, but the generic ABI does not specify this tie order. Do not encode two different intended behaviors under the same weak name and rely on a build system to preserve an incidental winner. An unresolved weak reference has value zero in the static link used here; a reference with a nonzero addend need not yield a field containing zero. The absence of a definition is also distinct from the presence of a definition whose value happens to be zero.

Visibility is a separate property from binding. A hidden global in one input can satisfy a reference in another input of the same output component; it does not become an ordinary dynamic export. A protected definition can be exported, but references inside its defining component bind there. Default visibility does not by itself promise an export or imply that a definition in a fully static executable can be interposed. In a shared object, however, a default-visible exported definition may be preemptible. Visibility constraints on participating references must propagate to the selected symbol; a non-default visibility reference cannot simply be satisfied by a definition in another shared component. Choosing a definition is not sufficient grounds for eliminating dynamic indirection.

