\section{Check a Pointer Table Against Its Definitions}

Use the following three handwritten x86-64 ELF assembly inputs. The table is inspected as data; there is no need to run the final image.

\begin{verbatim}
# survey.s: references use this object's symbol indices.
        .data
        .globl readings
        .weak optional_cal
readings:
        .quad sample_rate, optional_cal, tag
tag:    .byte 0x11

# fallback.s: this local tag is a distinct object.
        .data
        .weak sample_rate
sample_rate: .long 32000
tag:    .byte 0x22

# tuned.s: hidden across components, not across these inputs.
        .data
        .globl sample_rate
        .hidden sample_rate
sample_rate: .long 48000
\end{verbatim}

Assemble each input with \verb|as --64|. Inspect \verb|readelf -sW -rW survey.o|: GNU \texttt{as} may express the reference to the local tag with an input section symbol and an addend rather than the tag's own index. Link with \verb|ld.bfd -e readings -Map=survey.map| followed by \verb|-o survey survey.o fallback.o tuned.o|. Compare the three decoded 64-bit little-endian values from \verb|objdump -s -j .data survey| to the symbols in \verb|readelf -sW survey|. In order they must be the virtual address of the tuned definition (whose contents are 48000), zero, and the address of the byte \verb|0x11|. The third address must be exactly \verb|readings| plus 24.

\Cref{fig:visual-285} distinguishes the three binding outcomes before the table is written.

