Swap the last two objects and repeat: addresses may change, but the selected definition and the byte reached by the third pointer must not. Omit \texttt{tuned.o} and the first pointer must reach 32000. Turn the fallback's binding into \verb|.globl|, with both objects present, and require a multiple-definition error even though the other definition is hidden. For the weak tie, instead assemble a second weak fallback with value 44100 and reverse their order without the tuned object; inspect the selected contents, not just the symbol's name.

For a negative test, declare \verb|optional_cal| with \verb|.globl| instead of \verb|.weak| and leave it undefined. The same static link must now report the missing definition instead of writing zero. This isolates the binding of the \emph{reference}, as opposed to changing the strength of competing definitions.

The invariant is that a live relocation using an ordinary local identity continues to address that input's contribution, whereas a live non-local reference uses the prevailing definition under the link's binding policy, or the permitted unresolved-weak value. Neither a matching filename nor a matching printed name is a substitute for tracing the relocation to its actual target.
